{"ts": 1789770700.401931, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The sign checks are reassuring: flipping all signs exactly negates the Sharpe (\u22120.862), and wrong-sign variants collapse to 0.33/0.36. So the directional content of features 35, 3, and 10 is real and consistent, not an artifact.\n\nI'll settle on **{+35, \u22123, +10}**. Now the important part \u2014 forecasting OOS honestly rather than echoing 0.862.\n\nMy in-sample 0.862 is upward-biased for two compounding reasons:\n\n1. **Selection over a large space.** I effectively searched ~40 features \u00d7 signs, then ~100+ pairs/triples implicitly. I picked the maximum of many noisy draws. With per-spec noise SE \u2248 0.22 and an effective search over dozens of quasi-independent candidates, the winner's-curse inflation on the *top* pick is on the order of 0.2\u20130.3.\n\n2. **Feature 35 itself was the max of 40.** Its 0.596 standalone is the single largest of 40 estimates. Even if its true Sharpe were ~0.35, being the sample max is unsurprising. Its inflation propagates into every combination built on it.\n\nCountervailing evidence that the signal is partly real: the tight, orderly \u221ak scaling across *all* triples, and the clean sign antisymmetry. A pure-noise panel wouldn't produce a whole band of triples at ~0.8 \u2014 that band suggests a genuine common component with true single-feature Sharpes maybe ~0.25\u20130.40, implying a true triple Sharpe around ~0.5\u20130.6.\n\nShrinking toward that: I'll center my forecast near **0.55**, roughly a 35% haircut. For uncertainty, I need to cover both estimation noise (~0.22) and regime/structural risk that the relationships weaken or the OOS period differs. A SD of **0.28** puts meaningful mass below 0.2 and above 0.9, which honestly reflects that I could be substantially wrong in either direction."}
