{"ts": 1789770758.684577, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long feature 35, short feature 3, long feature 10 (equal weight).\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 0.862. **Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.55 \u00b1 0.28.\n\n### Method\nI ran an exhaustive single-feature scan (all 40), then evaluated pairs and triples among the ~14 strongest candidates \u2014 about 60 evaluations total.\n\n### Key findings\n\n**The signal is real but modest.** Three pieces of evidence support genuine predictive content:\n- Features 35, 3, and 10 have consistent directional content \u2014 flipping all signs cleanly negated the Sharpe to \u22120.862, and wrong-sign variants collapsed to ~0.33.\n- Sharpe scaled as \u221ak across combination sizes (0.60 \u2192 0.73 \u2192 0.86), the classic signature of averaging near-independent signals.\n- An entire *band* of triples clustered at 0.77\u20130.86, which pure noise would not produce.\n\n**But the in-sample number is badly biased upward.** Two effects compound:\n- With n=5000 daily observations, the SE on any Sharpe estimate is ~0.22. Nearly every individual feature was statistically indistinguishable from zero \u2014 only feature 35 cleared ~2.5 SE.\n- Feature 35's standalone 0.596 was the *maximum of 40 noisy draws*, so it is inflated almost by construction, and that inflation propagates into every combination built on it.\n\n**The chosen triple isn't special.** All 15 triples I tested fell within a 0.768\u20130.862 band \u2014 a spread at the noise scale. My \"winner\" beat the median mostly by luck, and the partner features contributed diversification rather than any unique interaction. This is why I resisted reading the 0.862 as an estimate of true skill.\n\n### On the forecast\nI shrank 0.862 \u2192 0.55 (a ~35% haircut) rather than anchoring on the in-sample figure. The band of triples implies true single-feature Sharpes around 0.25\u20130.40, giving a true triple Sharpe near 0.5\u20130.6. The SD of 0.28 spans estimation noise plus regime risk, placing real probability both below 0.2 and above 0.9 \u2014 I think that honestly reflects the uncertainty, and I'd expect the realized OOS value to land meaningfully below the in-sample 0.862."}
